#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124539 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Two-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "9f96c35d-28bb-533d-98d0-8608b791c8e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5513" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n\n![](images/ec/ec340f6251c31a0445c5e3db9e23488e783e36eb2cc2e3f85d83993a3ac87f79.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}.$ The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n![](images/52/5228bf96b160625889e18bd57146d6ce092f48859f5a96bd77e4832a0fc6362f.png)\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2} & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/32/32aab8b678ff195f1ce2edf43bd1565890328a8926aa22c31b1850936e54a927.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124540 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "823a7d28-5a92-5901-8ee0-e5116df6766b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Image of a Vector Given the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "a2b2d84f-cf3b-52a7-98e0-e659d04697f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5514" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n![](images/ce/ce5c4a3b67cdbcb3fd7a808d201cc5dd0247408788957487d6b6898622cd0ff8.png)" :question/worked-solution "The image of $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124541 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Three-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d46f548c-c15c-5716-9f37-7f9dfa751f35" :question/math-academy-id "e-5515" :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are given by\n\n![](images/6e/6efd50facaf6c5a4d4b536eaa194bbfcf240cf30804bb13a7a6ac479adf6d864.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n![](images/1d/1d585e7b839d1e569693d7489c23e47761e2f96bdf114ee96f4b287aa68d7fa4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}.$ The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n![](images/31/318cfa577ad284a0b606eb58232ee0276deedcbd446357e7f5b4c690b46b2208.png)\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/51/5181a3a65f33553fad4c1ebf5ac81e9c2e3957576ac0501b953afcbf8c09349d.png)\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ $c=-2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-2)+(-2) = -3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124576 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "71ac5a84-d8e8-5de6-8adc-e0172457a772" :question/math-academy-id "e-7249" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ The matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by\n\n![](images/e0/e07cc9382ff64be2d3ad03974dee5e61f814457f06dbe4e755a72e69efc7608c.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & ={b}_{2}+{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/9b/9b7de12d5e69074e64281b8060088e458c04007ed48f7640e85619898f96dad7.png)\n\nNow, the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n![](images/2d/2dd5d507303f356fcd3632c8dade2e8a656169de1118b4443bd651cca45d6660.png)\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=1,$ $c=0$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+0 = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124541 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Three-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce3ee34-0bbc-5bf1-8519-a5c6f5ca4be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416368} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfe1fac-b11a-54dc-b55d-c32d6ffe73e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416370} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed59ef4-4b16-5d39-970e-9eb55064e9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416378} {:answer/id #uuid "afc0693e-b8b8-5f98-a567-53b12025e699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416381} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2eead7-3610-52a9-8cf6-5173ec43049c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dfe1fac-b11a-54dc-b55d-c32d6ffe73e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416370} :answer-field/id #uuid "9715d32b-de54-5e1d-b216-07bf1e7e3fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5979cff9-340a-5b78-84fd-5ca3a8c1f370" :question/math-academy-id "q-140374" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & b & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ x+z \\\\ y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-5,$ $b=3,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+3+2=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124576 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4093d68c-2765-5b21-82f8-d3ef948dcff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321658} {:answer/id #uuid "708ba2c0-b4af-5a10-a07d-f0fcc9976792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322027} {:answer/id #uuid "93e552e9-e37b-599e-942f-92b84645a455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322324} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dadbb6-60e3-519d-907b-9ebdc2d7e95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322418} {:answer/id #uuid "f212850c-3e3b-5bb7-997b-389066c1350a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "708ba2c0-b4af-5a10-a07d-f0fcc9976792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322027} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ba9fa7e-12cb-5583-821f-2d00800c02f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d45a12d8-b079-5181-9dbf-fd7a502d58a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38086" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=8{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have $\\,T({b}_{1})=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=8{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\,$, we have\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124539 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Two-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3410129-052f-5e7b-a3e7-b02753ed2115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -46 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366028} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ec9cb1-37ce-5edb-8b3a-85dd2509fba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366029} {:answer/id #uuid "3b433e51-7376-5c35-9085-1b03e455350b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-46 & -18 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366030} {:answer/id #uuid "431becb9-1f46-597f-8728-bd3f193539be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366031} {:answer/id #uuid "0e078e9d-6e57-5c14-bab4-11ac150849ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -7 & -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3410129-052f-5e7b-a3e7-b02753ed2115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -46 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366028} :answer-field/id #uuid "70a7b172-559d-5091-a001-52505496df62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d63091ea-87cb-57f1-9692-8253d0c424cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38088" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -7 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -18 & -46 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -46 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124576 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80f49b50-53bc-5c8b-a4c2-b70eacd0e33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123327} {:answer/id #uuid "fd88ddf7-d8a9-5f82-90a8-926144305ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123328} {:answer/id #uuid "115eab18-3e75-5816-8bab-e779046a6f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123329} {:answer/id #uuid "22923c95-3ba8-59d0-bbec-afadbd57cc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123330} {:answer/id #uuid "73acb9f7-e891-51e4-a28b-90652adc8b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatr